 write final in polished style.\n\nCheck details for blackmail:\n\"An XOR blackmailer sends a letter iff exactly one of (i) rumor false and agent will pay on receiving letter, (ii) rumor true and agent will not pay. Given the letter, EDT pays because paying implies no termites. CDT refuses because termites are upstream. A-FDT refuses for a different reason: in the A-FDT graph, the letter is downstream of the predictor’s model of \\(D_{letter}\\). Setting \\(D_{letter}=refuse\\) makes receiving the letter unlikely, while leaving the infestation unchanged; setting \\(D_{letter}=pay\\) makes the letter likely and costs \\$1,000. Refusing has higher expected utility.\" Need source says CDT and Fiona refuse; yes.\n\nNeed maybe note \"in actual branch where letter received, A-FDT still refuses, because it evaluates the policy of being a payer, not the conditional news.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe add \"This illustrates updateless aspect.\" Good.\n\nPotential problem: In XOR blackmail if predictor perfect, if D_letter=refuse, letter is sent only when termites true and not pay? Wait letter condition: send iff exactly one true: (rumor false & pay) XOR (rumor true & not pay). If agent type refuses (D=refuse), then condition (ii) true when rumor true, so letter sent iff termites true. Thus receiving letter is bad news. If agent type pays, letter sent iff rumor false. If A-FDT evaluates do(D=refuse), it makes letter likely when termites, but doesn't change termites. Expected utility? If no letter no payment, if letter and refuse no payment but may have termites cost. Compare pay: if pay, letter iff no termites; pay $1000; no termite cost. Refuse: letter iff termites; no payment; termite cost maybe $1M? But termite cost occurs regardless of letter? The rumor/infestation cost occurs if true, independent. Paying doesn't affect infestation. So refusing weakly better because avoids $1000 when letter; but if letter sent only when termites, conditional on letter, refusing means you likely h